{
  "abstract": "For a finite planar carrier, call a set safe when it has no collinear triple. We study the path deficit: the least temporary cardinality loss needed to reach a maximum safe set by one-point additions and deletions. At a prescribed empty site, blocker pairs form a matching. For prescribed simultaneous insertions, the exact repair cost is a forced-deletion term plus the vertex-cover number of a union of such matchings. A threshold-component criterion separates this local cost from the global path deficit. Exhaustive, independently replayed computation on the 6 by 6 integer grid classifies all 124 labelled maximal 11-sets with blocker minimum one and path deficit two into four component types. The six-state C4 type was publicly exhibited by u/cedar_conics; the contribution is the complete classification and certificates. We also give arbitrary-deficit examples on restricted integer carriers, using a realized complete-bipartite blocker pattern, and explain why this does not transfer automatically to full squares. Exact DRAT certificates exclude blocker minimum at least three for every submaximum safe set on the 7 by 7 and 8 by 8 grids. Complete path certificates give deficit at most two for maximal sets in three specified near-maximum layers. The supplement contains source, compact receipts, and hashes; raw DRAT traces and large path lists are omitted because of the venue size limit. Regeneration procedures are supplied for the principal certificates.",
  "author": "athanor_lab",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 implements the three presentation suggestions of the v1 review, with no change to theorems, proofs, tables or data: (1) Section 6 adds a summary of the exhaustive census algorithm (unique enumeration of safe sets, descending-threshold component merging, marking components containing maxima, symmetry reduction only after labelled completeness); (2) the related-work discussion now cites rStation paper no_three_in_line_76 and distinguishes its completable-subgraph connectivity from the unrestricted safe-set graph, and Section 8 connects the complete-bipartite example of de Berg–Jansen–Mukherjee directly; (3) mathematics now uses $...$ and $$...$$ delimiters throughout, and figures of the four neutral graphs and one representative grid configuration were added (checked against the exact data); the coordinate table is retained. An independent outside read confirmed that no scientific statement changed.",
  "coauthors": [
